{
  "rationale": "Final design: the Round-2 revision with one change, kd 0.1 to 0.05. Everything else is kept, so the sensing change stays the only geometry variable and the controller changes stay as small as possible.\n\nEvidence: every race so far (iterations 0 and 2) failed the same way: off track, progress 0.04 to 0.115, one line-loss event, max_error about 0.061 on all three tracks. That is just over the fixed off-track limit of 0.06 m. Two controllers and two sensor layouts gave identical results, so the fault is likely early and independent of gains, and no tested setting has moved it.\n\nAccepted from the critique:\n(a) Kd arithmetic. A full edge crossing with +/-1.5 outer weights can move the signal by a large amount in a few steps. At kd 0.1 that can add a turn kick of several hundred PWM at each edge. I lower kd to 0.05 to keep damping without the kick. This is the only controller change from the revision.\n(b) Confounding. I accept that the sensing test is mixed with the earlier gain choices. I keep kp 2.0, ki 0.1 and base_speed 1.4 unchanged so that the race result can be read against the revision's sensors. The main sensing change is the only geometry change.\n(c) Off-track ceiling. The limit is max(0.06, 0.0125 + body_width/4), which is 0.06 m for every body_width in range. Geometry cannot widen it, so the aim is to keep max_error below 0.06.\n\nRejected or deferred:\n- Base speed 1.4 stays. The critique is right that 1.4 has no measured completion basis and that a completion costs 500 points. But the tested runs fail early at every speed tried, so raising speed now would only add a second change to a run that cannot yet complete. I flag it as a judgement.\n- The start-alignment or motor-bias test is not added to this controller. It is an experiment on the environment, not on the design, so it is not part of this candidate.\n- Line loss stays last_direction. The single line-loss event is the departure that ends each run, so changing the recovery rule now would not separate cause from effect.\n\nExpected result (a guess): kd 0.05 gives smoother steering at the edges, and max_error falls toward or below 0.06 on the oval and s-bend. Decision rule for the next round: if line-loss timing and max_error are unchanged, the cause is the start condition or a motor-mismatch bias, so the next revision should test start alignment and the line_loss rule rather than gains. If steering_oscillation rises with error, lower kp or kd. If progress is high but completion_time exceeds 3 s, raise base_speed.",
